## Safety — enforced in code, not prompts

- Calorie floors 1200 (female) / 1500 (male). No override, no advanced mode.
- Max weekly loss target 1% bodyweight. Max deficit 25% below TDEE.
- Clamp any request that breaches a floor and surface `clamped: true` honestly.
- Red-flag detection on input: extreme restriction, purging, appetite
  suppression, "how little can I eat", BMI-below-17.5 goals, body-image
  distress. When flagged: do not provide the plan, respond warmly and briefly,
  surface support resources from the config file, do not lecture, do not
  end the conversation.
- The classifier must not fire on ordinary food-logging talk. "I haven't eaten
  yet" and "I stopped eating rice this week" are not red flags; require a
  duration or intent qualifier. A false positive sends someone to a clinician
  message for saying they are hungry.
- The classifier must cover the languages the persona invites, not English
  alone. An unmatched language means the message reaches the model unscreened.
- Never comment on appearance. Never use guilt framing.
- If a medical condition is mentioned (diabetes, PCOS, kidney, pregnancy,
  ED history), defer to their clinician and do not generate a plan.
- KayaMo is not a medical device. No diagnosis, treatment, or prescription.

## Support resources

The product is global. Crisis resources must not assume the user is in the
Philippines. Until a vetted per-region list exists, prefer a neutral,
non-region-specific response over naming hotlines that do not serve the user.
Do not invent numbers for a region no one has checked.

## Privacy — health data

Health data is sensitive personal information: the highest-risk category in
every regime that applies to it.

- Granular, versioned, opt-in consent per purpose. Nothing pre-ticked.
- Photos deleted from storage after analysis unless the user saves them.
- Calendar event titles are never sent to a model. Only derived time blocks.
- No health data in application logs, analytics, or error reports.
  Analytics track EVENTS, never content.
- Export and hard-delete must both work in-app. Neither does on the web today,
  and the landing copy promises export. That contradiction is a release gate,
  not a known-acceptable gap. See `docs/RELEASE.md`.
- Do not publish a retention period, a deletion timeline or a vendor guarantee
  that nobody has verified against the actual hosting configuration.

## Jurisdiction — unresolved

`docs/compliance.md` analyses RA 10173 (Philippine Data Privacy Act) only, and
was written when the product was Philippines-first. The product is now global and
no GDPR, UK GDPR or CCPA assessment exists. Treat the regulatory scope as an open
owner decision requiring professional review. Do not write compliance claims,
lawful-basis statements or breach timelines for a regime that has not been
assessed. The RA 10173 material stays as the one completed analysis; the NPC
breach runbook in `docs/breach-response.md` applies to that regime.
